Overview
For starter project 3 assignment, I worked with my partner, Autumn Knox, to review each other’s Boggle solver code and implement changes based on given feedback. In this process, I created my own branch on GitHub, opened a pull request, reviewed my partner’s code as she reviewed mine as well, and gave necessary feedback. I utilized static analysis to ensure the changes I made on my code were still executable. 
The Boggle Solver game uses a grid of letters and a dictionary to search for words that can be formed. It looks at surrounding letters in eight directions and makes sure each tile is only used once when forming a word. Throughout the project, my main focus was to ensure the code was functional and organized in a way that is easy for others to understand.

Feedback You Received
My partner, Autumn, gave me a few suggestions on how I could improve my code. One suggestion she gave was to create a separate variable for the lowercase version of a word instead of changing the original word variable. She thought that this would make the code easier to read and I agree with her feedback. She also suggested moving some of the grid checks into its own function, which would make it more simpler to read and keep the validation separate from word searching. Additionally, I received complimentary feedback on my code. Autumn liked how I put the directions of the word search into one list instead of writing out separate code for each direction. She said that it made the search easier to follow and keep the code in an organized manner. She also liked the boundary check I wrote because it prevents the search from going outside the grid. The comments I added also made it easier for her to understand what part was being checked in the code.

Feedback You Gave
When reviewing Autumn’s code, I gave feedback on what could be improved and what I thought was done well. I like how she separated the row and column boundary checks instead of putting everything in one condition. Also, I liked how her variable names made the code easier to follow, such as row_change and col_change. Lastly, I liked how she listed all eight directions and included important comments throughout her search method. One of the suggestions I gave was to add setGrid() and setDictionary() methods so that the grid and dictionary could be updated more easily. Also, I noticed that she had an import re statement that wasn’t being used and main() was called twice at the end of her code, causing the results to print twice. Lastly, I suggested adding a few more comments throughout the code for the important parts of the game.

Improvements You Implemented
After reading Autumn’s feedback, I resolved a few suggestions she made. One major change I made was separating the grid validation from the main method so that the code was more organized. I did this by creating a new function named isValidGrid to check if the grid rows and tiles were valid. Another suggestion Autumn made was for me to create another variable for lowercase words. Although that was a good suggestion to keep the lowercase variable more clear, I decided to keep my original approach because it still worked well for my program. 

Static Analysis Results
For static analysis, the goal was to check the code for any formatting, style, and organization issues. I focused on variable names and any extra comments needed to make sure my code was consistent and easy for others to interpret. The improvement I implemented of creating the isValidGrid function was a great suggestion from Autumn as it allowed for me to keep the checking of the grid rows and tiles separate from my solution function. 

Regression Testing
For regression testing, the goal was to ensure that the changes I made did not disrupt the functionality of the Boggle Solver game. I utilized Codio as a tool to check my code after implementing the changes. My final test ran with 0 issues and this ensured me that the static analysis part of my work was successful and did not impact my code negatively

Reflection
Overall, this project gave me a better understanding on the stages of software development and how it is not just about writing the code. There is a process of planning the code, writing, testing, peer review, static analysis and regression testing. I’ve learned the importance of peer review as code can be written many ways and produce the same output, yet hearing input from other developers is a great way to learn more and gain new ideas for your code. I also got more practice using GitHub to review code, leave comments, and approve pull requests. One thing I learned is that code reviews aren't just about finding mistakes. They're also about giving suggestions, explaining why something could be improved, and recognizing what someone did well. I think this is important because when working with other developers, everyone has different ideas and approaches, and being able to give and receive feedback is part of working together. 

